{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/861651-primacy-2e","id":861651,"title":"Primacy 2E","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-04-08T00:08:05-07:00","id":861651,"title":"Primacy 2E","content":"So i'm trying to make a 2E update for the primary system from domination city. I have an idea of how to work out input, assets, resources, and loyalty/protection. <br /> But I can't think of how to change agent without making it retainer. Do you guys have any ideas for something that?<br /> <br /> What I got so far as running ideas are.<br /> <br /> Influence<br /> can be raised with purchases of social merits having to do with human society. Such as contacts, retainers, allies, status. But not things like fast talker or Iron Will. <br /> Along with the enigma skill application of influencing organizations from Dark Era. Maybe giving a new kind of Beat to go along with this like a Primacy Beat.<br /> <br /> Input<br /> This is the representation of how much fucks your Prince give about your opinion. So you can use this as bribes to other vampires for when the Prince calls for ideas. furthermore in order for you to influence the Prince and convince him to take your idea will be a chase like system using you input as the amount of time you can roll.<br /> <br /> Assets <br /> for this I leanings towards it being more than a bunch of skills that happen to have names.<br /> instead what I have in mind is that contacts, retains, allies, status, and other merits to represent people. But I don't want this to be the end of it. I'm thinking of letting you buy equipment to further boost some assets, like a surgeon's kit for a back alley doctor for example. While also letting you purchase other kinds of merits like \u201ctolerance for biology\u201d for that same doctor. Of course these merits dots would have to be tagged to a certain asset making them more valuable but also a larger target.<br /> <br /> Resources<br /> I'm renaming this to investment to showcase the wealth that's both yours and not. I want this to be the amount of resources that you can call upon from your assets having a risk when doing so.<br /> <br /> Loyalty/Protection<br /> I want to remove the loyalty aspect from this, mostly because of True Friend being a core book. While also changing the name of protection to soldierly turning it more militant in nature, by giving them more rolls then protect this defend that.<br /> <br /> Agent<br /> ???","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10820-blakemikizuki","name":"Blakemikizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10820_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"280"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-04-10T23:55:39-07:00","id":863505,"title":"","content":"So this is what I got for input.<br /> <br /> <b>Input</b><br /> In Primacy, the Prince, surrounded by would-be counselors and advisors \u2014 the players\u2019 characters among them, sits at the top of the city. When a course of action comes before the court \u2014 such as a criminal charge against one of the city\u2019s Kindred, a proposition from a local mage, or the choice to attack or entreat a burgeoning Carthian revolution \u2014 the Kindred advisors, whispering in the Prince\u2019s ear or conferring with him in chambers, make their case in secret. One advisor gets his way. Influencing the Prince is a special action, similar to the Chase System called \u201cInput\u201d. There is one large difference between a chase and input, the prince only has so much time to listen to a single vampires case. So each vampire is limited to a number of rolls determined by their input. You do not need to attempt to persuade the prince on the first night many vampires wait it out, researching the topic in question and try to bend it to their own benefit.<br /> <br /> Input Modifiers<br /> Circumstance Modifier<br /> <br /> your Idea has hints of the <br /> prince's opposed beliefs. +1<br /> <br /> Your Idea has heavy traits of <br /> the prince's opposed beliefs. +2<br /> <br /> Your Idea is the opposed beliefs <br /> of the prince's. +5<br /> <br /> Idea has hints of the <br /> prince\u2019s beliefs. -1<br /> <br /> Idea is similar to the <br /> prince's beliefs. -2<br /> <br /> Idea are almost identical<br /> to the prince's beliefs. -3<br /> <br /> Your character knows <br /> the topic. -1<br /> <br /> Your character knows the <br /> topic intimately. -3<br /> <br /> Recently performed a favor <br /> for the prince. -1<br /> <br /> Your character has the <br /> backing of others. -1 to -5<br /> <br /> Political sabotage. +1 to +3<br /> <br /> Informed one day late. +1<br /> <br /> Informed week late. +2<br /> <br /> <b>Set the Terms</b><br /> First, determine the course of action that comes before the court. Such as a criminal charge against one of the city\u2019s Kindred, a proposition from a local mage, or the choice to attack or entreat a burgeoning Carthian revolution. This determines the terms of the input \u201cChase\u201d.<br /> Start with the initial options to choose from, as well as what it would take for any vampire to to convince the prince to make that choice. The players can submit additional options via their characters by defining a course of action. These traits reflect the number of successes each vampire needs to convince the prince. <br /> The base number of successes for a given character to prevail is five. Modify that with the following variables as examples, but feel free to make up your own. Use only the highest modifier for a specific category. Also note that each character has his own target number here. Note that this target number can change from turn to turn. Smart players can manipulate their character\u2019s circumstances in their favor. <br /> When a vampire achieves the required successes, he sways the Prince to her plan, and that is the course of action ordered by the Prince. The character gains one Influence point per success. If no vampire reaches the required successes, then the use his own preferred plan successfully and the input ends. Vampires should be weary at this time for the prince may feel that those advising him need to be replaced.<br /> <br /> <b>The Edge</b><br /> The Prince plays favorites, every vampire knows this. So it's up to every would be councillor to find the prince's preferred opinion.<br /> As the context changes from turn to turn, as the prince gains a wider understandings of the situation. The Prince determines what dice pool has the Edge any turn, and any vampire that finds this edge better chance of success. In terms of the narrative, the character that can spin his speech just right can more easily sway the prince to his plan. Player should work with the Storyteller to determine what that means. Often, preparation can influence this; if you can choose the topic, you can pick a discussion with an inherent advantage. If you can\u2019t easily determine who has the Edge at a given moment, make a contested roll. Usually, this will be based on Wits or Manipulation. The relevant Skill should relate to the context. Often, this will be political, Streetwise, Persuasion, or similar.<br /> <br /> <b>Turn-by-Turn</b><br /> Input turns reflect half a night's worth of game time; At the beginning of every turn, the terms of situation is explained at the beginning of input. The Storyteller secretly determines the Edge the dice pool for the input that turn. or if a vampire has gained the prince's favor in the last turn, then that vampire\u2019s has the edge. This dice pool has to make sense within the current context of the situation and the prince's ideals. This allows player to find the princes beat to play the situation to their favor.<br /> <br /> If any vampire uses a different dice pools then the edge dice pool, they lose the 10-again quality on his roll. If they continue to press suffer a cumulative -1 penalty. This penalty increases every time he uses the same dice pool instead of reaching for more ideas. He does not suffer it on turns where he has the Edge or otherwise uses the dictated dice pool, but it continues to amass if he decides not to on a future turn. Note that the side with the Edge rolls first; this means if they hit the required successes, they get their desired outcome for the input before the other side makes their roll.<br /> <br /> <b>Counsel and Sabotage</b><br /> This is the basic action for Counsel and Sabotage in a input scene. <br /> <b>Dice Pool:</b> Special, determined by the side with the Edge. The Storyteller should determine relevant bonuses and penalties based on the situation. <br /> <b>Action:</b> Instant <br /> <b>Dramatic Failure:</b> Your character suffers the terms for failure that turn. This can include the effects of failure, as well as a Condition. <br /> <b>Failure: </b>Your character suffers the terms for failure that turn. You can lose one combined success from your total, or your character suffers a specific narrative effect such as a Tilt, or a loss of one willpower. <br /> <b>Success: </b>Your character overcomes the immediate hurdle in his attempts to persuade the prince, and makes headway. Calculate successes, and add them to your running total. <br /> <b>Exceptional Success:</b> In addition to a massive accumulation of successes, your character manages to set back the opposition. You can subject one opposed vampire to the effects of a failed roll, regardless of his roll\u2019s outcome.<br /> <br /> <b>Gaining Favor \u201cSeizing the Edge\u201d</b><br /> To add a slightly deeper engagement, offer players the choice to \u201cSeize the Edge\u201d during each turn of the chase. After the side with the Edge determines the rolls for that turn, both sides predict their rolled successes that turn, and jot them down. Put these numbers face down on the table. As the dice are rolled, uncover those numbers. If a player successfully guesses their rolled success, that player gets the Edge during the next turn, despite dice and circumstance. This rule heavily favors underdogs because it\u2019s easier to guess successes with fewer dice; it\u2019s a good option to mix up play if the players are trying to out maneuver a well established elder in politics.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":10820,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10820-blakemikizuki","user":"Blakemikizuki","date":". 04-11-2016, 03:15 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/10820-blakemikizuki","name":"Blakemikizuki","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar10820_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"280"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}